// Explanatory study aids. Slide references identify the underlying deck concepts.
export const minibatchAdvantages=[
 {title:'Cheaper updates, without waiting for the whole dataset',mechanism:'A full-batch update processes every training example before changing the parameters. A minibatch estimates the gradient from a subset, so the model can start improving after much less work per update.',example:'With 100 examples and a batch size of 10, one pass through the data gives 10 parameter updates. A full-batch method gives one update after all 100 examples.',tradeoff:'An epoch still visits all examples. The benefit is cheaper, more frequent updates, not automatically less total work or faster convergence in every case.'},
 {title:'More data per update has diminishing returns',mechanism:'A larger batch can give a less noisy gradient estimate, but each added example costs computation. Once the estimate is useful enough, processing more examples before updating may add little benefit relative to that cost.',example:'Under independent sampling with finite gradient variance, quadrupling the batch size reduces a coordinate’s gradient variance to one quarter and its standard deviation to one half. It does not make the estimate four times as precise in standard-deviation terms.',tradeoff:'This is the deck’s “no linear return” idea. The value of a larger batch depends on the data, hardware, and stage of training; larger batches can still be useful.'},
 {title:'Parallel computation keeps the GPU busy',mechanism:'Examples in a batch can be processed together using matrix operations. GPUs have many computing units, and batching gives them enough similar work to execute efficiently in parallel.',example:'Instead of predicting one example at a time in a loop, a matrix operation can produce predictions for a whole batch. The examples share the current weights, then their gradient contributions are averaged.',tradeoff:'Increasing the batch can improve hardware throughput up to a point. GPU parallelism is finite, and a batch that is too large can hit memory limits.'},
 {title:'A manageable batch fits in memory',mechanism:'Backpropagation needs intermediate activations as well as inputs. Processing a subset limits how many of those intermediate values must be stored together.',example:'If a model can hold activations for 32 examples but not for the whole dataset, a minibatch of 32 still lets it train on the entire dataset over successive updates.',tradeoff:'Weights and optimizer states also consume memory. Smaller batches reduce the batch-dependent portion; they do not make an arbitrarily large model fit.'},
 {title:'Hardware-friendly batch sizes can improve throughput',mechanism:'GPU kernels work on blocks or tiles of numbers. Batch dimensions that fit those blocks can reduce padding and improve utilization. This motivates trying convenient sizes such as 32, 64, 128, or 256, as suggested in the deck.',example:'Two nearby batch sizes can have different examples-per-second rates because of how well their matrix dimensions fit the hardware’s execution pattern.',tradeoff:'A power of two is a practical starting point, not a requirement or a guarantee. Kernel choice, GPU type, precision, and the other matrix dimensions affect the best size.'},
 {title:'Small-batch noise can act as implicit regularization',mechanism:'Different batches usually produce slightly different gradients. That variation perturbs the optimization path and can affect which solution is reached, sometimes improving performance on unseen data.',example:'One batch may favor increasing a weight while another favors decreasing it. Training follows a noisy sequence of proposals rather than the exact full-dataset gradient at every step.',tradeoff:'This effect is called implicit regularization because it changes training behavior without adding an explicit penalty to the loss. Better generalization is possible, not guaranteed; excessive noise can make training harder.'},
 {title:'Control the extra variance with the learning rate',mechanism:'A smaller batch generally gives a more variable gradient estimate. Since SGD multiplies that estimate by the learning rate, lowering the rate reduces the size of both the estimated movement and its random variation.',example:'For a fixed gradient estimate, halving ε halves the parameter change. If the estimate includes an error term, its contribution to the update is also halved.',tradeoff:'This is the cost of the small-batch advantage, not an extra benefit. Too small a rate slows useful progress. Choose batch size and learning rate together; a rate reduction is not a universal rule for every configuration.'},
 {title:'Shuffling makes successive batches more representative',mechanism:'If examples are ordered by class, source, or time, consecutive batches may repeatedly push the model toward one part of the dataset. Shuffling spreads those patterns across batches and reduces the systematic dependence caused by the ordering.',example:'Suppose all cat images come before all dog images. Without shuffling, early batches emphasize cats and later batches emphasize dogs. Shuffling usually creates a more balanced sequence of updates.',tradeoff:'Shuffling does not make draws without replacement strictly independent, and it does not remove dataset bias. Time-series tasks may also require preserving temporal structure. The goal here is to reduce harmful order effects in ordinary shuffled training.'}
];

export const challengeConnections=[
 {challenge:'A full-dataset gradient is expensive',method:'Minibatch SGD',lesson:3,slide:18,also:[0],mechanism:'Estimate the gradient using a small subset, then update without waiting for the entire dataset. This makes individual updates cheaper and more frequent.',limit:'The estimate is noisier; fewer examples per update do not guarantee lower total training cost.'},
 {challenge:'Updates zigzag across a narrow valley',method:'Momentum',lesson:4,slide:20,mechanism:'Keep some of the previous velocity. Alternating sideways contributions can cancel, while consistent movement along the valley accumulates.',limit:'High momentum can overshoot, and the effect depends on the learning rate and the landscape.'},
 {challenge:'Momentum carries movement toward an overshoot',method:'Nesterov momentum',lesson:4,slide:23,mechanism:'Evaluate the gradient at the momentum lookahead position. The slope there supplies a correction informed by where the old velocity is taking the parameters.',limit:'Lookahead changes the measurement point; it does not guarantee that every step avoids an overshoot.'},
 {challenge:'A cliff produces an enormous gradient',method:'Gradient clipping',lesson:1,slide:8,mechanism:'Cap the gradient magnitude before the update. Norm clipping shrinks all entries by one factor, preserving direction while limiting the gradient contribution.',limit:'Clipping does not restore a vanished gradient or guarantee a useful descent direction. With momentum, stored velocity can also affect the final update.'},
 {challenge:'Signals shrink or grow repeatedly across layers',method:'Glorot or He initialization',lesson:2,slide:13,extraSlide:14,also:[1],mechanism:'Choose the random starting weight scale to help preserve signal magnitude. Glorot uses fan-in and fan-out; He accounts for rectifier nonlinearities such as ReLU.',limit:'This is a starting-scale intervention, not a complete solution for long-term recurrent dependencies or unstable gradients throughout training.'},
 {challenge:'Parameters receive gradients at different frequencies or scales',method:'AdaGrad',lesson:5,slide:24,mechanism:'Give each coordinate its own denominator based on accumulated squared gradients. Frequently active coordinates build larger denominators; rarely active coordinates retain relatively larger effective rates.',limit:'The accumulator never forgets. Its growth can eventually make updates very small.'},
 {challenge:'AdaGrad’s long history shrinks updates too far',method:'RMSProp',lesson:6,slide:26,mechanism:'Replace the cumulative sum with a moving average of squared gradients. Old large gradients gradually lose influence, so the denominator can adjust to recent conditions.',limit:'Tiny updates can also reflect small current gradients or a small base learning rate; inspect the cause before changing optimizers.'},
 {challenge:'Gradients are noisy and coordinate scales differ',method:'Adam',lesson:7,slide:28,mechanism:'Average signed gradients to estimate direction, and average squared gradients to estimate scale. Normalize the corrected direction estimate using that scale.',limit:'Averages smooth variation, but can lag changing gradients. Adam is not guaranteed to outperform SGD or generalize better.'},
 {challenge:'Adam’s early moment estimates are pulled toward zero',method:'Bias correction',lesson:7,slide:29,mechanism:'Divide each raw moment by 1 minus its decay raised to the step count. This compensates for starting the moving average at zero under the moment-estimation model.',limit:'This corrects moment initialization; it does not fix biased training data or a poor loss function.'},
 {challenge:'Updates remain too large near a useful solution',method:'Learning-rate decay',lesson:8,slide:34,mechanism:'Reduce the learning rate over training, making late adjustments smaller and reducing the size of gradient-noise contributions to the updates.',limit:'Decay too early or too far can stall progress. A schedule does not identify a minimum or guarantee convergence.'},
 {challenge:'Early full-sized updates are unstable',method:'Warmup',lesson:8,slide:42,mechanism:'Begin with a small learning rate and gradually increase it. This makes the initial updates gentler while the model and optimizer states begin adapting.',limit:'Warmup does not repair every initialization, data, architecture, or numerical problem.'},
 {challenge:'A saddle or flat region gives little gradient signal',method:'Stochasticity and existing momentum',lesson:1,slide:7,also:[3,4],mechanism:'Minibatch variation can perturb the trajectory away from some saddles; an existing velocity can carry movement through some low-gradient regions.',limit:'Escape depends on local geometry and noise. At an exact saddle with zero velocity and no gradient noise, first-order methods can stay put.'},
 {challenge:'A poor starting region or local minimum limits progress',method:'Initialization, different starts, and learning-rate restarts',lesson:2,slide:11,extraSlide:40,also:[1,8],mechanism:'Initialization and different random starts explore different initial regions. Raising the rate at a cosine restart can encourage broader movement later in training.',limit:'A learning-rate restart resets the schedule’s clock, not the weights. None of these methods guarantees the global minimum.'}
];

